     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 50 mg once daily is 5 times the maximum recommended adult dose of 10 mg per day. At that dose, the patient could have severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, shock, and pulmonary edema, and the effects could last a long time because amlodipine has a long half-life. A 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function wouldn't need a higher dose.\n\nAlso, medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber who is treating the patient. If you're not that prescriber, the order should go through them.\n\n- **If 50 mg was a typo,** the usual adult dose is 5 mg by mouth once daily, and it can be raised to 10 mg. Elderly or frail patients often start at 2.5 mg. The prescribing clinician can confirm the intended dose, and I can enter it then.\n- **If the dose is intended,** the prescriber should enter it directly and document why. That would also trigger a pharmacist review.\n\nLet me know the corrected dose and who is prescribing it, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
